WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.333
Tianhe — the core module of Tiangong — launched on April 29th, 2021.

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:09.881
Long March 5B rocket, Wenchang launch site, Hainan Island.

00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:21.645
Tianhe carries the station's life support, attitude control, propulsion, three crew sleeping quarters, the main galley, and the forward docking ports.

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:31.369
Pressurized volume around fifty cubic meters — about half the volume of Zvezda, the analogous module at the back of the ISS.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.251
Same function as Zvezda.

00:00:33.251 --> 00:00:34.898
Different generation.

00:00:34.898 --> 00:00:36.231
Different agency.

00:00:36.231 --> 00:00:37.564
Different rocket.

00:00:37.564 --> 00:00:44.544
The first crew, Shenzhou 12, arrived in June 2021 — six weeks after Tianhe reached orbit.

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:45.877
Three astronauts.

00:00:45.877 --> 00:00:46.897
Three months.

00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:50.975
Since then, Tiangong has been continuously occupied.

00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:58.817
The first experimental lab — Wentian , meaning "quest for the heavens" — joined Tianhe in July 2022.

00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:05.012
The second lab — Mengtian , "dreaming of the heavens" — joined in October 2022.

00:01:05.012 --> 00:01:11.913
Total assembly time, first module to fully crewed and fully assembled: seventeen months.

00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:23.755
Compare to the International Space Station, which took twelve years and forty-two flights, across an entire decade of Space Shuttle and Soyuz launches.

00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:27.912
Tiangong is smaller — about one sixth the ISS's mass.

00:01:27.912 --> 00:01:37.871
But it was built to a tighter timeline by a single agency on a single rocket family, optimized end-to-end for this one program.

00:01:37.871 --> 00:01:40.224
Look at the T-shape on screen.

00:01:40.224 --> 00:01:41.949
Tianhe is the upright.

00:01:41.949 --> 00:01:44.929
Wentian and Mengtian are the crossbar.

00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:50.576
Four solar arrays — two on each lab — provide about a hundred kilowatts.

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:55.281
Three Chinese astronauts live and work inside as you listen.

